.TH MINSEC 1 "2026-08-24" "minsec" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
minsec \- minimalist log-driven intrusion prevention daemon and control utility
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B minsec
.RI [ options ]
.I command
.RI [ command-options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B minsec
watches selected log files or the systemd journal for authentication failures
and other abuse.
It tracks failures by filter and source network, then asks a configured backend
to ban sources that reach the retry threshold within the configured time window.
.PP
The default
.B nft
backend maintains the
.B inet minsec
nftables table.
Ban expiration is implemented by nftables set timeouts, so active bans survive
daemon restarts and expire without daemon intervention.
.PP
The same binary provides the foreground daemon, offline configuration and
filter inspection commands, and commands that communicate with the running
daemon through a Unix-domain control socket.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c " " \fIdirectory\fR ", " \-\-config-dir " " \fIdirectory\fR
Use
.I directory
as the configuration directory instead of
.IR /etc/minsec .
The directory contains
.IR minsec.toml ,
.IR conf.d/ ,
and
.IR filters/ .
.TP
.B \-\-json
Request machine-readable JSON from commands that support it.
For
.B test
without
.BR \-\-quiet ,
one JSON object is written per matching line; with
.BR \-\-quiet ,
a single summary object with line, match, and per-address counts is written
instead.
.B events
always emits its stored JSON Lines records.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the program version and exit.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS "daemon [--backend backend] [--replay]"
Run the daemon in the foreground.
The backend override may be
.BR nft ,
.BR null ,
or
.BR exec .
It changes the loaded configuration only for this process.
The
.B null
backend records what the engine would do without changing the firewall.
.PP
File sources normally start at end of file.
.B \-\-replay
starts existing file sources at the beginning.
Journal input continues from the journal follower's normal starting point.
.SS "check [--all]"
Load and validate the merged configuration and compile filters.
By default only enabled filters are compiled.
.B \-\-all
also compiles every discovered built-in and custom filter, including disabled
custom filters.
This command is offline and is suitable for checking changes before a restart.
.SS inspect
Inspect the merged configuration, discovered files and filters, and effective
per-filter policy.
Human-readable output is a short summary; use
.B \-\-json
for the complete, versioned inspection document.
This command is offline.
.SS "test filter [file] [--quiet]"
Compile
.I filter
and run it against
.IR file .
If
.I file
is omitted or is
.BR \- ,
read standard input.
By default each match shows the extracted address, pattern number, and input
line, followed by a summary on standard error.
.B \-q
or
.B \-\-quiet
suppresses individual matches and prints per-address totals instead.
.PP
This command tests textual matching only.
It does not apply retry thresholds, allow lists, IPv6 prefix aggregation,
escalation, or firewall actions.
.SS status
Ask the running daemon for its version, backend, uptime, counters, and effective
policy and source type for every enabled filter.
.SS list
Ask the running daemon for active local bans and their remaining lifetimes.
The
.B nft
backend is asked for kernel truth.
The
.B exec
backend cannot enumerate bans, so the daemon answers from its own in-memory
mirror, which only covers bans issued since it started.
.SS "ban net [--ttl duration]"
Manually ban an IP address or CIDR network.
Host addresses are normalized to a /32 or /128 and CIDRs are truncated to their
network address.
Without
.BR \-\-ttl ,
the configured default
.B bantime
is used.
The daemon refuses networks that overlap its effective allow list.
Manual bans are not used as multiplayer reports.
.SS "unban net"
Remove an address or network from the local ban backend and forget its current
per-filter retry state.
.SS filters
List embedded filters and custom
.I filters/*.toml
definitions.
An asterisk marks an enabled filter.
.SS "enable name"
Validate that the filter exists and set
.B enabled = true
in
.IR conf.d/name.toml .
Existing settings in that drop-in are preserved.
Restart the service to apply the change; the daemon does not live-reload
configuration.
.SS "disable name"
Set
.B enabled = false
in
.IR conf.d/name.toml .
The same restart requirement as
.B enable
applies.
.SS "events [-n count]"
Print the last
.I count
records from the event log, oldest first.
The default is 50.
The log is
.I events.jsonl
in the state directory together with the single rotated generation
.IR events.jsonl.1 ,
which the daemon creates when the current file passes 8 MiB; both are read and
older records are gone.
Records are emitted as JSON Lines and include daemon start/stop, automatic and
manual ban, and unban events.
.SH DURATION SYNTAX
Durations may be bare seconds or one or more integer/unit pairs.
Units are
.BR s ,
.BR m ,
.BR h ,
.BR d ,
and
.BR w .
Examples are
.BR 30 ,
.BR 10m ,
.BR 1h30m ,
and
.BR 2d .
.SH FIREWALL BACKENDS
.TP
.B nft
Owns only
.BR "table inet minsec" .
It creates local ban sets
.BR ban4 / ban6 ,
allow sets
.BR allow4 / allow6 ,
crowd-feed sets
.BR crowd4 / crowd6 ,
and input and forward base chains at priority \-10.
Allow rules precede drop rules.
The daemon requires permission to execute
.BR nft (8)
and administer nftables, normally
.BR CAP_NET_ADMIN .
.TP
.B null
Performs no firewall changes and is useful for evaluation and filter tuning.
.TP
.B exec
Runs the shell command configured by
.B defaults.exec_command
with one of these argument sequences:
.nf

setup
ban net ttl-seconds
unban net
.fi
.PP
The configured string is evaluated by
.BR /bin/sh " " \-c ,
and the arguments above are appended as positional parameters instead of being
substituted into the text.
The child process is equivalent to:
.PP
.nf
/bin/sh \-c 'exec_command "$@"' minsec setup
.fi
.PP
A command may therefore be a script or program path, or a shell fragment that
reads
.BR $1 ,
.BR $2 ,
and
.BR $3 .
It must be trusted configuration.
This backend does not enumerate active bans or install an allow set; the daemon
still refuses to issue a ban for a network overlapping its allow list.
.SH CONTROL SOCKET
Online commands use the socket configured by
.BR paths.socket .
The daemon creates it with mode 0660 owned by the user and group it runs as, so
online commands must run as root or as a member of that group.
The protocol is newline-delimited JSON: one request and one response per line.
Supported request command names include
.BR ping ,
.BR status ,
.BR list ,
.BR filters ,
.BR ban ,
and
.BR unban .
Use the CLI or
.B minsec inspect --json
instead of depending on human-readable output.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MINSEC_LOG
Logging filter for daemon mode.
The default is
.BR info .
Values use the
.B tracing-subscriber
environment-filter syntax; for example,
.B minsec=debug
enables debug logging for minsec targets.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/minsec/minsec.toml
Main daemon configuration.
.TP
.I /etc/minsec/conf.d/*.toml
Lexically ordered configuration drop-ins.
.TP
.I /etc/minsec/filters/*.toml
Custom filters and overrides for embedded filters.
.TP
.I /run/minsec/minsec.sock
Default control socket.
.TP
.I /var/lib/minsec/events.jsonl
Persistent event log.
.TP
.I /var/lib/minsec/events.jsonl.1
Previous generation of the event log.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/minsec.service
Packaged systemd service unit
.RI ( /lib/systemd/system/minsec.service
in the Debian package).
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns 0 when the requested operation succeeds and 1 for configuration,
filter, control-socket, backend, or other operational errors.
.SH EXAMPLES
Validate all known filters before restarting the daemon:
.PP
.nf
minsec check --all
systemctl restart minsec
.fi
.PP
Test the sshd filter without changing the firewall:
.PP
.nf
minsec test sshd /var/log/secure
.fi
.PP
Observe the full effective configuration:
.PP
.nf
minsec --json inspect
.fi
.PP
Create a one-day manual ban:
.PP
.nf
minsec ban 198.51.100.7 --ttl 1d
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR minsec.toml (5),
.BR minsec-filter.toml (5),
.BR minsec-sync (1),
.BR minsec-sync.toml (5),
.BR nft (8),
.BR systemd.service (5)
